WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.400
Tom Hanks is known as the nicest guy in Hollywood,&nbsp;
but his new novel is about an actor who's kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:10.140
of a nightmare. Surprisingly, Hanks says the&nbsp;
character is based at least partially on himself.

00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:13.740
That's right: even America's&nbsp;
dad has bad days, apparently.

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:17.520
According to Hanks' new interview&nbsp;
with the BBC, he doesn't always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:21.420
succeed at being the nicest guy in the&nbsp;
room. While promoting his first novel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:25.320
"The Making of Another Major Motion Picture&nbsp;
Masterpiece," Hanks said that the arrogant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.240
actor in the story is partly inspired by some&nbsp;
of his most stressful days on set, revealing,

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.680
"I have pulled every single one of those&nbsp;
moments of behavior myself on a set. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:39.720
everybody is at their best every single day on a&nbsp;
motion picture set. I've had tough days trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.220
be a professional when my life has been falling&nbsp;
apart in more ways than one and the requirement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:48.660
for me that day is to be funny, charming&nbsp;
and loving — and it's the last way I feel."

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:53.220
The book focuses on the drama behind the&nbsp;
scenes of a wildly expensive superhero film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:58.380
from a megalomaniac director to the aforementioned&nbsp;
difficult actor. The actor constantly keeps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:02.580
causing delays to the filming with his bad&nbsp;
behavior. Obviously, this is an exaggerated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:07.620
version of Hanks' real on-set behavior, but in any&nbsp;
case, he's admitting that he isn't always perfect.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:11.640
"That rug was given to me by the Dalai Lama!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.360
And you sent it in for cleaning?&nbsp;
You're off the picture!"

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.980
The Academy Award winner did say there's one&nbsp;
rule he never breaks about on-set behavior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.420
Unlike the fictional actor in his book,&nbsp;
who struggles with punctuality and causes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.580
problems when he eventually arrives on set, Hanks&nbsp;
makes sure he's never tardy for a shoot. He said,

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:34.200
"What cannot occur on a motion picture is that&nbsp;
someone cannot monkey around with the timing or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:38.580
the length of the shoot or the budget. That is&nbsp;
a cardinal sin in the motion picture business.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:42.240
You will be amazed at how many people&nbsp;
know that they can get away with it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:45.960
and are told they can get away with it, because&nbsp;
they are carrying the movie on their shoulders."

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:50.460
It's not surprising that someone as established&nbsp;
as Hanks, who has been a major part of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:55.680
and TV industry for decades, has some internalized&nbsp;
rules he'll never break. He's only human, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:00.300
and sometimes his demeanor slips. It's&nbsp;
sometimes for good reason, though. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:04.920
example when some fans were chasing Hanks and&nbsp;
his wife Rita Wilson in the summer of 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.920 --> 00:02:10.620
one tripped Wilson. Hanks was enraged, jumping&nbsp;
to the defense of his wife and shouting,

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.880
"Back the f--- off!"

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:16.500
Even with the occasional bad day for Hanks, it&nbsp;
doesn't seem to have translated to him losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:21.120
work. He's one of Hollywood's most prominent&nbsp;
and consistently cast actors. Notably,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.800
he won two Oscars in a row for "Forrest Gump" and&nbsp;
"Philadelphia," and he hasn't slowed down since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:31.140
working with directors like Steven Spielberg&nbsp;
and Ron Howard regularly. Regarded as one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.400
the best living actors, Hanks is also one of&nbsp;
the highest-grossing performers working today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:39.480
so obviously, directors are willing to&nbsp;
overlook a few bad moments here and there.

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:43.920
In 2022 alone, he appeared in&nbsp;
three high-profile projects — Baz&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.120
Luhrrman's biopic "Elvis," his frequent&nbsp;
collaborator Robert Zemeckis' adaptation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.120 --> 00:02:51.840
of "Pinocchio," and the heartwarming&nbsp;
family flick "A Man Called Otto."

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.640
"Do you think I'm going to cut myself, and&nbsp;
bleed all over your floor and sue you?"

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:00.240
He's also dabbled in television&nbsp;
documentaries in the last several years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:06.300
producing 2019's "The Movies" and "1968:&nbsp;
The Year That Changed America." He also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:11.340
worked with "Yellowstone" head honcho Taylor&nbsp;
Sheridan on an episode of the spin-off "1883."

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:16.140
Hanks isn't going anywhere any time soon, and&nbsp;
if you want to check out a dramatized version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:20.940
of his on-set behavior, "The Making of Another&nbsp;
Major Motion Picture Masterpiece" is out now.

